  {
   "id": "math-0016",
   "bucket": "reasoning",
   "planned_path": "P5",
   "scion_answer": "We have an unfair coin where $P(\\text{heads}) = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ and we flip it 5 times. We want the probability of exactly 2 heads.\n\nThis is a **binomial probability** problem:\n\n$$P(k \\text{ heads}) = \\binom{n}{k} \\cdot p^k \\cdot (1-p)^{n-k}$$\n\nwhere:\n- $n = 5$ (number of flips)\n- $k = 2$ (number of heads we want)\n- $p = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ (probability of heads)\n- $1 - p = \\dfrac{1}{3}$ (probability of tails)\n\n**Step 1:** Compute the binomial coefficient:\n$$\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{120}{2 \\cdot 6} = 10$$\n\n**Step 2:** Compute the powers:\n$$\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{4}{9}$$\n$$\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^3 = \\frac{1}{27}$$\n\n**Step 3:** Multiply everything together:\n$$P(2 \\text{ heads}) = 10 \\cdot \\frac{4}{9} \\cdot \\frac{1}{27} = \\frac{40}{243}$$\n\n**Answer:** $\\boxed{\\dfrac{40}{243}}$",
   "scion_ok": true,
